Working on this over the past two weeks, it got much more complicated than I expected and ended
up using elements of every coding class I've taken thus far, though I'm proud of it.

I've included the three features described in my Assignment #2 submission. 

Adding a Habit: As a user, I want to enter a new habit name and frequency so that I can begin tracking a new routine.

Onboarding: As a user, I want a step-by-step setup guide so that I am guided through my first goal creation without confusion.

Dashboard: As a user, I want to see a list of my habits so that I can assess my daily progress at a glance.

IH#1 — Explain the Benefits of Using New and Existing Features
The landing page explicitly lists what the app offers before the user commits to anything — minimalist 
interface, daily check-ins, progress visualization, and local data storage. The onboarding Step 1 also 
lists exactly what the user will accomplish in the next 60 seconds. Abi and Pat can assess value immediately; 
Tim can see the "Coming Soon" sidebar to find what's new and interesting.

IH#2 — Explain the Costs of Using New and Existing Features
The onboarding tells the user upfront that setup takes 60 seconds. The landing page clarifies that data 
is saved locally and never shared, addressing privacy concerns before they arise. Abi and Pat can make 
an informed decision before investing any time.

IH#3 — Let Users Gather as Much Information as They Want, and No More Than They Want
The onboarding Step 1 sidebar showing "New Features" and "Coming Soon" is optional — Tim can read it, Abi 
and Pat can ignore it and just click Next. The edit modal's "View raw data" collapsible works the same way — 
power users can expand it, casual users never need to.

IH#4 — Keep Familiar Features Available
The dashboard uses universally recognized conventions — a + button to add, a chevron (›) to open details, 
and a progress bar for completion status. These patterns are consistent with iOS, Android, and popular 
productivity apps like Apple Reminders. The delete button, though greyed out in Sprint 1, is placed where 
users would expect it.

IH#5 — Make Undo/Redo and Backtracking Available
When a user clicks Back on the Add Habit form after typing a name, a confirmation panel appears warning 
them they may lose unsaved work. They can either confirm and leave or return to the form. This gives Abi 
and Pat confidence that accidental navigation won't destroy their work, and gives Tim a safety net when exploring.

IH#6 — Provide an Explicit Path through the Task
The three-step onboarding with a labeled progress bar ("Step 2 of 3"), a filling pill at the bottom, 
and clear Next/Back buttons gives Abi a structured, unambiguous path through first-time setup. Tim and 
Pat can skip onboarding on return visits since localStorage remembers completion and sends them directly 
to the dashboard.

IH#7 — Provide Ways to Try Out Different Approaches
There are three separate ways to add a new habit — the + Add button in the nav bar, the empty state button 
when no habits exist, and the Add Habit page reachable from onboarding. This means Abi who follows the 
explicit path, Pat who explores a bit, and Tim who clicks wherever they want will all find a way to the 
same action.

IH#8 — Encourage Tinkerers to Tinker Mindfully
Required fields are marked with a red asterisk before the user attempts to submit. If they try to save without 
a name, a red error message appears immediately and blocks the save — preventing bad data from being created. 
The back-out confirmation modal adds a second checkpoint before data is discarded. The "Custom" option on frequency 
and duration lets Tim enter freeform values, but the field is clearly labeled and bounded so they understand the scope 
of what they're entering.